Z||000025||william_thorniii||02-13-2000||03:28 AM||||OZ,<P>IRM doesn't have a web site so to speak.  There is a site that I can send you the URL to on Monday where someone scanned in their flyer.  The guy calls himself Fiero Master, but I have no idea if you will find it on a search.  <P>Duke Lovers,<P>I recently was referred to a website that sells many performance parts for the Duck... er... I mean Duke.  I will also gladly post the URl on Monday.  These are all in my favorites on my work computer.  I don't keep anything on this peice of $#!^.<P>Good luck,<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||10
Z||000026||Ozzy||02-14-2000||07:25 PM||||Hey Willy! Wheres the URL's???<P>And Kidzwheelz...For all that positive reinforcement stuff like 'Where there's a will there's a 4 banger turbo' you went kinda quiet on me, just when you got my hopes up!! Tell us if you found anything!!<P>Curious Ozzy||||reg||1
Z||000027||william_thorniii||02-14-2000||08:12 PM||||OZ,<P>Patience, patience...<P>Here is the URL: <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/MotorCity/Lane/1588/quickcat.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/MotorCity/Lane/1588/quickcat.html</A> <P>It is only a scanned catalog.  It is close to being up to date.  Remember just because it is not in the catalog does not mean that they can't get it for you.  I am interested in this topic because I am another owner of a notorious Iron Duke and have considered the home fabrication of a turbo Duke.  <P>Everyone else,<P>The correct size for our engine should be a Garett T2 or T2.5.  These are the same that were used on all chrysler 2.2L's, Chevy 2.0L's and Ford 2.XL's.  Either would work fine, but I would prefer the T2 since it's smaller impeller has less inertia and therefore would reduce time to "spool".  And you don't ever have to worry about running out of turbo with a Duke.  They will run out be over compressed long before the turbo stops breathing.  <P>William T. Thorn, III ||||reg||10
Z||000028||HF6582||02-14-2000||09:43 PM||||Just a note on the Pfaff turdo. When they designed the system they had to add two extra injectors. The TB injector on 84-86 models are rated at 58lbs. If you do the math that is just enough for 100hp, so you are looking at increasing fuel pressure or adding more injectors for a turbo system.<P>They also added a secondary computer which fired the injectors with manifold pressure reached the + side, with the OEM ECM runnning the engine systems. Kind of a piggyback unit.<P>I'm not saying that it cannot be done, but it is going to take some engineering on your part.<P>Scott||||reg||1
Z||000029||Cooter||02-14-2000||09:51 PM||||It is funny that you would mention that about the extra injector. I have a friend that was trying to build a stand alone analog/digital "computer" just for applications like this. His used a map sensor and took readings from the O2 sensor also. When map went to the + side and o2 went lean, this would fire an extra injector to keep the mixture from going lean. We kidded him about using a second fuel supply and running model airplane fuel in the injector with the boost up- methanol with castor oil and 35% nitromethane. He never did get all the bugs worked out of the system. If I see him again, I will ask how it finally turned out. ||||reg||1
Z||000030||kidzwheelz||02-14-2000||09:56 PM|||| Hey Oz, I'm still here man! Just to let you know what I'm thinking, I'm looking at the possibility of using the turbo system off of a Pontiac Sunbird. They were 2.0 liters, and while not the same as our 2.5 dukes, they are close enough in size that the possibility exists that the electronics could be made to work with our engines. The only trouble I'm having with this right now is locating one. They were built in the early nineties, but I'm not sure yet which years they were available. All of the resident experts I know seem to agree with me that this is a REAL possible fit. I will keep you posted on any progress, and until then I am educating myself on turbos, because like I said before, its not my strong suit!  All forum members, keep the info coming. You just don't realize yet how helpful it has been.||||reg||7
Z||000031||Ozzy||02-14-2000||10:29 PM||||Kewl Dewd!! Right Arm!<P>So we know it's gonna need more juice. What about a modified single injector?? (I'm thinking about the 700 TBI too. These were on the '87 & '88 Duck.) Maybe a larger Injector from another type of throttle body all together. Also, what of the vacuum controlled stuff like, er, brakes(???), EGR and MAP sensor. The lines for the EGR and MAP come from the vacuum ports on the Throttle body base on the 700. <P>I've heard some about turbo units like needing oil lines and water cooling lines. Are there units out there that don't need this stuff. Would make for easier installation? <P>I know, I know! These are probably dumb questions to all you 6cyl, turbo engineer, rocket scientist guys but a guy can dream can't he?? (Can't he?????)<P>Just remember the Wright brothers all you 4 cyl fanatics! <P>Optimistic Ozzy||||reg||1
Z||000032||FIEROCIOUS||02-15-2000||06:09 AM||||Kidzwheelz, resident expert? Thanks, I knew I was good but expert? Oh your not talking to me? LOL J/K<BR>Anyway....The part name that I couldn't remember earlyer today was a Boost referenced (or riseing rate) fuel pressure regulator.<BR>Now fieroites what this is, is a fuel pressure regulator that goes up with boost pressure, (up to approx 7-10 psi) with this little gem there is no need for a ECM remap.<BR>A dual map ECM is better but this will work just the same!<BR>Dual map: The computer in the Fiero is a speed densidy system it uses set formulas called tables to meter fuel and timeing. The problem with this is it ONLY works at atmostpheric pressure. The computer needs a completly new table to adjust for Boost pressures. <BR>A Map is a grid chart so dummies like me can understand the numbers that the computer uses for its inputs.<BR>P.S. Thanks Kidzwheelz for your help today. I don't think the hood would have fit in my Fiero! I need to get a truck!<BR>||||reg||10
Z||000034||88 Fiero||02-15-2000||02:13 PM||||Kidz..back in the early '80's...A company used a sunbird turbo 2.0 liter on a fiero and start selling it as a complete car...the sunbird will fit without a problem...just rerouting the hoses..and I believe they used to call it the Andretti GT...||||reg||1
Z||000035||Phantom Rage||02-15-2000||02:41 PM||||Ok I agree with using a smaller turbo unit, but the one off the sunbird is too small.  Yes if you use it you will get quick spool up, but you would be on the verge of boost on Idle.  The trade for this quick spool up is massive backpressure, high compressor/turbine speeds, which lead to high heat, less life.  A size like a t25 a.k.a.: (t2small) may be true with high rpm motors, but will work just fine with the dukes 5g redline.  I too am working on a turbo project; so far I have a mitsu tdo5-14g turbo from a starion/conquest modified to a 16g with a clipped turbine for less back pressure.  This turbo will have quick spool up but will flow equivalent to a t3.  Find an intercooler if possible, I found one from an 87 Peugeot 505 Turbo S.  Don't get one too large as this will cause lag, and make it hard to fit in the engine bay.  A rising rate fuel pressure riser is a good way to go, but limited to lower boost (under 9psi) levels Vortec makes a good one called their FMU (fuel management unit).  I don’t really know if you need a new higher flow fuel pump, but if it is the same one used in the 2.8 motors they it should work just fine, if not I’m sure you can get a high flow one.  Well that covers your fuel assuming that your are gonna use low boost levels.  What about spark then,  MSD makes a boost-timing master, this should work for 84-86 dukes, but not 87-88 dis dukes.  I e-mailed MSD a while back and they said they were working on a unit, should come out in about a year (not sure when but that was some time ago.) This in not to be confused with their digital dis-2 system.  The dis-2 system will retard timing, but it is rpm dependant, not boost.  I guess it might work, but I am no engine management expert.  A blow off valve is also a good Idea, this keeps the boost form creeping back into your turbo, and extending it's life, with quicker spool up, when your tbi butterfly is closed.  You can pick one up in a junkyard, look for mopar 2.2L turbo 2 motors; they have them inside their air filter boxes (these air boxes are black, not the silver ones and sit sideways not upright).  You can either just let the expelled air go out into the atmosphere, which will make that cool ssspppppphhhhhhh, noise or you can route it back into you TB intake line and make it stealthy.  I also got a new map sensor form a turbo sunbird; this one is set for 2-bar boost good up to 16psi, I think.  I am no engine management expert, my question to those who are is: since the Map sensor controls the majority of engine timing off of vacuum, could I assume that when I went into boost, this lower the vacuum and thus retard the timing? (Referring to 87/88).  Some one stated earlier about a turbo that uses no oil or coolant lines.  You could use one of these turbo's but their life is limited.  To get oil you could tap into your oil pressure sender, you don't need high flow or high pressure.  Make sure your oil return line is slightly large that your oil feed line for a gravity effect (to use this your turbo must be above your oil pan), tap into your oil pan now and fit the return line above the highest level of where the oil sits level.  It really doesn't matter where you get you coolant from, I am thinking of tapping into the thermostat housing neck below the thermostat itself, both feed and return lines, if possible.  You should also use steal braided lines for both oil and coolant lines, secure these as they will chew up anything it rubs against.  Some other things to consider, is downtime, price, always-using high-octane fuel, synthetic motor oils.  I know you have rebuilt your motor but I’m betting you still have over a 9:1 compression ratio, this means low boost levels, unless you lower it, but this would not be fun when not on boost.  You should also invest in some gauges, a lean/rich gauge is very important, boost level gauge would also be a good Idea.  A cool option is using turbonetics manual boost control, you can control your boost form inside the car, and this way you don’t have to mess with the wastegate diaphragm rod.  Do beware of the boost gods, though.  I am gonna stay at 6psi, I know this is low, but I don’t have a built up motor, Yes I know I am an Idiot for doing so, but remember I have an intercooler which should help.   Ready for another stupid idea, but cheap.  I am using the stock exhaust manifold, cutting the stock flange and having the proper flange welded to it.  These manifolds are not really strong and will probably crack; so make sure if you do like wise, support your turbo to the engine block.  Use a good size down pipe 2.5/3 inch, for quicker spool up.  Unlike naturually aspirated motors, bigger is better when it comes to exhaust.  Just another cool aspect of a turbo: it will quiet your exhaust note a good bit.    <P>These are just my thoughts and once again I stress I am no engine management expert, so please correct me if I am wrong or have better suggestions.  I would hate to lose my duke due to some stupid mistake I made.  <BR>Good Luck!!!<P><BR>[This message has been edited by Phantom Rage (edited 02-15-2000).]<p>[This message has been edited by Phantom Rage (edited 02-15-2000).]||||reg||7
Z||000036||terryk||02-15-2000||05:56 PM||||Could you edit that and insert some paragraph breaks?||||reg||7
Z||000037||Phantom Rage||02-15-2000||06:24 PM||||Sorry!  Just excited.||||reg||7
Z||000038||Ozzy||02-15-2000||11:37 PM||||Damn Phantom!!! Almost a thesus you have there!!<P>Sounds like you have done some home work. How much do you have into this project so far? What purpose does the intercooler serve in the turbo system? <P>I was the one wondering about the oil and cooling issue. However the plan to connect the oil and cooling supply sounds relativley simple the way you described. Could the oil return be tapped into the rocker cover?<P>Is your Duke an '88? Mine is and additionally, the engine has a recent rebuild on it but not with high performance parts. Standard crank, rods and pistons. It has been bored 40 thou. over also. I have headers instead. The pipes seem smallish so do you think the stock manifold would work better? <P>Have you thought about the vacuum assisted brakes and the affect of possitive pressure on the master cylinder? Also what about the EGR?? (I think you mentioned a solution for the MAP sensor)<P>Please keep us posted on your progress. I'm dying to know how it turns out. <P>Inquisitive Ozzy!||||reg||14
Z||000039||Phantom Rage||02-16-2000||09:42 PM||||Mitsu turbo: $45 from a junkyard<BR>16g + Clipped turbine wheel + rebuild: $400<BR>Intercooler: $17 I knew the guy at the junkyard: he rang it up as a heater core. Hehee.<BR>Turbonetics blow-off valve: $100, Just my luck, I find out about the mopar ones afterwards, which would have only cost $5.<P>The intercooler works just like a radiator, but for hot compressed air.  The cooler the compressed air, the less detonation, and thus higher boost levels can be achieved.  A water-cooled intercooler would work best for a fiero, but they cost a lot, plus there are other things involved, which makes it just a little bit harder to install.  <P>I'm guessing the rocker cover is just another name for the valve cover.  If this were the case, you would have to mount the turbo above the valve cover.  This would be hard due to room and design of the exhaust manifold.  You could add a pump, but that's just one more thing that could break and spell disaster.  <BR>Remeber gravity is on your side.  The turbo should be mounted above where you plan to tap the return line (once again use a larger line than the feed line). The turbo does not require a high flow of oil, just some to keep the bearings happy.<P>Yes, my duke is an 88.<P>I have a header too, 4-into-1 hooker.  I thought about modifying the header, but for the price I could have a real turbo manifold made.  I figure I could sell it and get some money back, I just have to get it ceramic coated again.<BR>The stock manifold isn't all that bad for naturally aspirated, stock dukes.  The stock manifold probably isn't the best Idea and would prove a bit restrictive, but I am trying to keep prices low.<P>Wow, I haven't really though about the egr or the brake system.  Thx for the advice, I will try to figure that one out.<BR>I'm not sure about the map sensor either, I was hoping one of you experts would be able to aid. <P>I forgot to mention there are piggyback computer systems out there.  Two I have heard good reviews about, in the turbo crowd.  Accel and SDS, SDS seems to be the favorite.  From what I understand you don't need a laptop, it comes with an small monitor GUI; which you can control your fuel maps and spark curves.  I can not afford the $900+ price tag though.<P>Good luck too all who modify or just plain love their Fiero's.<P>||||reg||7
Z||000040||Ozzy||02-16-2000||10:42 PM||||Hey Phantom...<P>Does not sound like your into it too hard on the bucks side. <P>I was thinking you had a solution for the MAP then I re-read your book 2 posts above to find you were asking for help...Not getting a lot of input from others and I'm not a lot of help. Please keep posting though. This is the closest I've seen here of somebody willing to persist in 'Breathing life into the Duck'.<P>The MAP sensor has the largest orifice in the small manifold on the throttle body. It also has that orifice positioned above the butterfly when in a closed position. I should imagine if it has the range from whatever inches of vacuum to 5 or 6 psi, it could just be tapped into a point just before the mouth of the turbo intake. I think the EGR would need choked as it utilizes much less vacuum. Probably experimentation. <P>I'm certainly no turbo engineer either. Just thinking out loud.<P>Ozzy||||reg||1
Z||000041||kidzwheelz||02-17-2000||09:38 AM||||  O.K. The Pontiac Subird we need to locate was built from 87 to 90; it was a 2.0 liter with engine code M. The Sunbird was also available from 84 to 86 with a turbo, but it was based on a 1.8 liter with an engine code of J. I have found a very raped 86, but I'm still hoping to locate one of the 89 or 90's.<P>  Another possibility exists also. I have spoken to a couple of places about setting up an ECM with mass air and knock sensor systems. They didn't tell me that they couldn't do it, but they also haven't told me they can. They are looking into it. I will sit on the name of the place for now, but if they tell me they can do it, I will make it forum knowledge and give you guys their number.<P> More to follow!!!||||reg||1
