Z||000062||theogre||02-22-2000||06:36 PM||||Cooter, others, The ECM acould drive 2 injectors in parallel pretty easy. I don't know what the current rating is of the driver transistor. There are several ways it could be done.<P>1. the OEM driver can handle the extra load. In this case it shouldn't be hard to wire. hard part is making sure the injectors are balanced pretty close.<P>2. the transistor can't handle extra load. in this case adding 2 driver transistors after the first is probly easiest. You'd need 2 because the transistors ad a very slight delay. you want both injectors to fire as a unit. This isn't very hard to do really. It can even be done external to the ECM I believe. This is probably the best of the options. I can think of off hand.<P>The 200 IAC I think is electrically the same as the 300's is. same for the TPS. I still can't find my book.||||reg||1
Z||000063||88formula||02-22-2000||08:10 PM|||| TURBO PUKE||||reg||1
Z||000064||batboy||02-22-2000||08:27 PM||||Hehehe! Once you guys get your 2.5 built up and turbocharged after spending as much for it as a good performance engine swap, I'll pull two spark plug wires off the Batmobile's tired ol' 350 and still blow you away. Sorry to disappoint you boys, but turbocharged or not, you still won't have much low end torque and if you try to make a lot of horsepower on the top end, the engine won't last long. However, I do admire your hotrodding spirit and your "never say die" attitude! Don't let me talk you out of it though. Go for it and prove me wrong!||||reg||1
Z||000065||Cooter||02-22-2000||10:38 PM||||I would love to get about 120 HP out of my DuckMobile, but to me it aint worth the trouble and lack of durability. My Honda engine is rated at 110 HP and is going strong at over 130,000 miles. I cant see a turbo/high performance duke lasting that long and be as trouble free. No flames please! Everybody needs a grocery getter- I cant afford to drive the 'Burb anymore and the Honda gets 10 more MPG than the Fiero. I hope you guys can get your turbo project on the road. <BR>More food for thought- late model s-10 trucks have a DIS 2.2 multi point fuel injection system. Maybe the injectors, harness and ecm might provide the fuel service you are looking for.||||reg||1
Z||000066||Ozzy||02-22-2000||11:22 PM||||Gentlemen...and Batboy (LoL. Just kidding!) It seems to me that this thread, an age old 'no way, no how' argument is fast aproaching the hydraulic clutch thread status!! I.E. Some real possibilities and interest have been generated which is very cool.<P>A super duty would be cool, I agree, but everything I have read tells me this will cost anywhere between $3,500.00 to $7,000.00 depending on the engine you buy/build. I got too many other things this money can be used for!!! <P>As for transplanting another 'heart'...granted, you might reap the benifits of BIG torque and HP but also at substantial cost and downtime IF you have somewhere to store your project.<P>I don't think anyone seriously wanting to turbo their 'duck' is looking to pull 9 G's on the first test lap. And so far it seems some solutions have been found and the parts list cost does not seem to have exceeded around $600.00 mostly collected from boneyards.<P>"Why turbo a DUCK" some may ask? Well, some people build performance bicycles, skateboards, mopeds(??).<P>Terry-k, theogre, cooter, fierocious and others have provided some very constructive ideas here and I think it's a blast!! <P>fillasofical Ozzy!||||reg||14
Z||000067||kidzwheelz||02-23-2000||02:14 AM||||  Way to keep the spirit alive, Oz! The reason I WILL turbocharge my duke is because of the people who say it can't be done and that it's a waste of time, blah, blah, blah.<BR> When I started this thread, I was just asking if any of you had done it, or seen one that was done. Now, because of some of the responses I have received, I'm going to do it just so that I can post the pictures and time slips to show all of you that it can be done, and is maybe worth the time and money to be different. If all I wanted to do was go fast, I would drop in a 350 TPI. I just want to do something different. Is that a bad thing??? I am having the kind of trouble I expected finding easy to acquire, bolt in parts. But, like I mentioned before, if I have to put a carb on it to turbocharge it, it will be done!!! Stay tuned!||||reg||12
Z||000068||FIEROCIOUS||02-23-2000||04:32 AM||||Batboy, I (used to be) the fastest street car in my area, I had a 73 Chevy nova with a 350. It did high 11's in the quarter at sea level it would do 10's. It had a mild cam idled great and got good gas miledge until I swapped the rear end gears for 4.10's I raced the same people weak in weak out they would do all sorts of stuff and spent loads of money to beat me while I with my engine that cost 500.00 to rebuild stayed on top for 3 years. To name a few cars under my whoop @ss belt: 502 chevelle, 383 blown chevy II, 440 road runner, and even my favorite a 427 blown chevelle with slicks! (This was illegal street raceing)<BR>Why only 3 years? I sold the car, I constanly was pulled over and it got OLD!<BR>I,ve sence did the honda thing, I'ts definatly not a 350! I learned how to build fast V8s from the nova, I now am an expert on EFI's from the Civic and will soon know more than I did about turbos. If I stuck to 350's my whole life I'd be like every other red neck and scratch my head when a fangdangled injection car came in! I am always learning, and know more than most men who are four times my age! Try new things be creative blaze a few trails! If the first HotRodder desided that "the factory builds em best I won't mess with it" we'd all be driveing econo boxes! <BR>And besides 350's are like @ssholes every ones got one and they all stink!<BR>Long live high output good gas miledge 4 bangin fun!<BR>P.S. map =M.A.P. sensor!   ||||reg||10
Z||000069||batboy||02-23-2000||07:13 AM||||I wasn't putting anyone down or bad mouthing the duke, one of my Fieros has the 2.5 and I used to love getting 35 mpg with it when I commute. I'm going to rebuild or stick another duke engine back into it, but it ain't no hotrod. Turbos are a great way to increase the power output and I wish you guys all the luck on your projects. I'm sure the knowledge gained by doing this with be beneficial, whether the motor holds up in the long run or not. Back in my teenage years I hotrodded my 56 VW. I had Okraska heads, dual carbs, tuned exhaust, the works. I sure could blow away any other VW, but those "assholy" small block Chevys still blew me away. I like the "stink" of a 350, even if that makes me a redneck. I guess I don't notice the smell that much since I'm usually ahead of the pack...LOL. By the way, the VW engine kept throwing rods, probably just like the 2.5 will do eventually. You can hotrod anything to the max and get some great time slips while it's fresh, but will it last in the long run?. After you have about 80,000 miles of thrashing your turbo 2.5 and everything is still ok, THEN I'll be impressed. I never said it can't be done. I really hope it works out. It's just that I'm skeptical. Just look through the Forum for a while, I've been wrong about stuff in the past. Like I said earlier, prove me wrong this time. Go for it and good luck.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000070||88 Fiero||02-23-2000||12:01 PM||||I could understand everyones concerned for the need of speed or who has the fastest car...But did anyone ever thought of reliability once you do all this mods..or the money that's going to cost you to build one of this turbo units...not only that if it fails..will that cost again to rebuild your engine and start all over again with the project and spend money again...for all of that expense you could have bought a turbo charge car that came from the factory...Anyway..just a thought...I love my car...but now I have a new toy...2000 Celica GTS...to drive around...but my fiero will be my heart and soul...ooopppsss!! shouldn't say that or my GF will kill me!!!||||reg||1
Z||000071||Ozzy||02-23-2000||01:46 PM||||O.K. I think we've established the pro's & cons of the object. Some say "yeah" and some "Ney". Moving beyond all that it's obvious that some will persist whatever they find on the other side of the mountain!<P><BR>Now for another twist...(You there Roy??) A gentleman from the forum here (Not sure what his forum handle is) was nice enough to scan and e-mail to me a copy of an '84 Hot Wheels article on modifications to a 'Duke" Fiero done by Smokey Yunick. It incorporates a turbo unit but is not really 'Turbocharged" pursea. Confused?? Good! It is a fuel mixture pre heating concept done in stages to create something of an environment to maximize fuel burn. According to this article most of the fuels power/work producing properties are wasted under normal atomization techniques.<P>The article claims some pretty astounding numbers in HP if you buy into the general consensus surrounding the 2.5L Duke engine. 250 HP in fact with the only internal mod to the engine being a cam shaft change and this was only to increase exhaust flow to use the heat from the exhaust for the final preheating stage. And if your not "pooh poohing" yet, they also claim this kind of HP with economy of around 50mpg!!!<P>"Why the hell didn't this system hit the market in kit form then?" would be your obvious next question. I don't know and asked the exact same question. As I remarked to Roy, maybe the petroleum industry joined with the mafioso and fitted the production engineer with cement booties!!<P>Anyway, I have a copy of the scanned article and will happily send it to anyone who is interested. It's a little hard to read and is in a Word '97 format but gives the general idea. (Thnx again Roy!)<P>Ozzy||||reg||1
Z||000072||Phantom Rage||02-23-2000||03:13 PM||||Wow!!! Ozzy what have you done?  Man, I come back and cheek the forum and so much info.  I'm glad that Kidzwheelz and Fierocious are making great progress.  Good work guys.<P>Hey, incase anyone is gonna some how use the stock computer the 2 bar map sensor for the turbo sunbird needs the sunbirds wiring harness connector, the duke's will not work.  They look the same but the sunbirds map has some plastic tab guides in them.<P>Talked to some local sorces and found a ecm programer, well atleast a friend of a friends.  I will have to see how that turns out.<P>Please keep up the good info guys.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000073||FIEROCIOUS||02-23-2000||03:16 PM||||Batboy I was kiddin (you know that)<BR>As for the reliability and cost.<BR>Power is not the only concern with the turbo up grade. Economy will be only slightly affected, cost will be less than a swap, and reliability will be the same as the (naturaly asperated duke) How? We are not going to boost the HE!! out of it just 7-10 psi. It will have a dual map so it will still run if the tubo fails, as for price of a rebuilt ready to install turbo it's 150.00 plus the cost of the turbo 50.00, so we are not talking big money! like I said the most problem we are haveing is the computer and the intake (sealing it against atmostphere) So if we can concentrait some effort on these two areas we can push on and make it happen. <BR>"There is no point to make a track burner when all I drive on are streets!"<BR>"Think of it as a street burner!"<P>P.S. Smokey Yunik since he retired from raceing has devoted a large portion of his life to makeing fuel efficient power! I firmly belive in any thing this man has done.<BR>If you read on his exploits you will be better for haveing done so!||||reg||10
