Z||000086||kidzwheelz||02-28-2000||09:12 PM||||  Oz, dude, I can't even begin to tell you how close I am to making this work. I have almost got the entire system figured out. (With the help of a couple of other forum members) <BR>  Right now I don't want to give out any false info, so I have slowed way down on the posts. Fierocious and I are going to a local yard tomorrow to acquire a few more pieces for his V-6 and my Duke. <BR>  When I do get mine totally figured out, I will post all the info along with the step by step documentary of my project.<BR>  There are still just a couple of things still to be dealt with, like whether to stick with the TBI, or to Multi-port the bad boy. I may have a solution to both these scenarios.<BR>  I myself contacted IRM, but the people I spoke with were less than helpful. To the best of my knowledge, they have some equipment that may be very useful, but the problem seems to be that not everyone on the IRM staff has been given the "cluebird"! On two occasions I have called and on both occasions was told the person I needed was not there at the time.<BR>  I will continue trying to get IRM involved in this, but in the meantime, I have found a couple of people who seem a bit more willing to help out. I want to thank all forum members again for remaining positive and wishing us well in this little endeavor. I hope you all find some pride and admiration in our success. ||||reg||12
Z||000087||theogre||02-28-2000||11:53 PM||||Yes, I'm talking about using the 200 TBI. It's very much the same as the 300 TBI you already have except you need to make an adaptor for the manifold and a drive for the injector. The 200 will deliver more fuel and air than a 300 ever will under any conditions. It flows 500+ CFM stock to a V8.........<P>you might ought to consider boxing the TBI. This would give you boost compensation to the fuel pressure regulator wich would make some of your fuel map easier. ((You might even be able to run a near stock fuel map))Either that or braze/epoxy a port on to the regulator air vent and feed it boost. only thing you have to watch if you braze a port is that you don't draw a vacuum in the fuel reg or you'll dump all the fuel down the return line. ((Vac in the reg would pull the reg completely open. No fuel pressure at all.))||||reg||1
Z||000088||SCCA FIERO||02-29-2000||12:20 AM||||Here are some of my ideas even though I have a V6. This may be of interest to FIEROCIOUS.<P>I have a Y pipe that is ment for an auto Fiero. Problem: it mounts the turbo right where the slave and shift cables are... not gonna work. What I have thought of doing is mounting the turbo towards the rear of the engine bay and making all new exhaust. I guess the exhaust would go down into a 3 inch cat. then across to the passenger side of the car to give room for a muffler then putting a single tip on the passenger side. All pipe for exhaust will be 3 inch. I then need to make a pipe going from the Y pipe to the turbo. I have a downpipe that I can make work and has provisions for the O2 sensor. <P>I would like to plumb the intake to the original airbox to get air from outside the engine compartment. Haven't figured that out yet.<P>My next problem is the electronics. I guess the MSD BTM (Boost Timing Master) is the best way to go (any other ideas?). Then there is the fuel problem. What is the best way to get more fuel to the injectors? And can the stock injectors supply enough fuel? I guess the options are 1) a rising rate fuel pressure regulator and 2) reprogram the PROM. Which is the best/easiest way to go?<P>I think Tom is gonna force-feed his V6 as well and I KNOW I want to. I think there are enough people here to get this done. terryk, orge, kidzwheelz, FIEROCIUOS and turbose can all be helpful in this quest of horsepower. Let's keep this going and get it done!||||reg||1
Z||000089||FIEROCIOUS||02-29-2000||04:43 AM||||Riseing rate is kinda a pain but works well, I think the way to go is definatly a prom Kidz is working on a ECM  from a quad four that utilizes a knock sensor and this is were I slightly disagree. This may be the only way though, I think it is needless cost. A MSD Knock sensor linked with a boost retard would be easier but I have been proved wrong before!<P>As for the V6 IRM as far as I know still sells the turbo prom. Also check whats his names page to find were he got his V6's prom programed or E-mail him.<BR>Let me know what you find.||||reg||1
Z||000090||Raydar||02-29-2000||05:01 PM||||Regarding IRM...<BR>There was a post, a few weeks back, that said that IRM was having a bit of difficulty due to the owner (Jim, I think is his name) having had a stroke. He is lucky to be alive, and is recovering, but they have all they can handle, to keep several businesses running. As I understand it, Jim's main business (and source of income) is electronics. The IRM/Fiero thing is a secondary business. <BR>They are spread pretty thin, right now. I'm sure they are doing the best they can.||||reg||1
Z||000091||Eric||02-29-2000||05:02 PM||||Hey I thought this thread was only about the Duke.  I really need to turbo my V6, but I'm not sure how to go about it.  Can I assume that all the manual transmission configurations have the same dimensions and the shifter cables are in the same place?  If so, we could design a system that would fit all our cars.  I was hoping to add an intercooler also as if things weren't already confusing enough.  My 2.8 is actually a 3.1.  I'm not sure of the compression ratio yet, but I'm going to find out.  Other than that, the motors should have about the same fuel requirements etc.  I already have an adjustable fuel regulator; however, I don't think it will suffice with a turbo.  19lb injectors from a mustang are probably in order.  ||||reg||1
